"CONSUMER CONTRACTS\n\"good faith\" stated in Uniform Commercial Code § 1-201(b)(20). See also Restatement of the Law\nSecond, Contracts § 205, Comment a (AM. L. INST. 1981). In consumer contracts, good-faith\nperformance and enforcement have been interpreted by courts as the promotion of a faithfully\nagreed common purpose that is consistent with the justified and reasonable expectations of the\nconsumer. As drafters, businesses sometimes seek to include discretionary terms that allow them\nto determine the specifics of their performance obligations, change the price, and modify or adjust\nother obligations under the contract. See § 5, Comment 3. Courts have enforced terms that\nestablish discretionary obligations but have relied on the duty of good faith and fair dealing to limit\nany actions inconsistent with consumers' justified and reasonable expectations and the common\npurpose of the contract. See, e.g., Fagerstrom v. Amazon.com, Inc., 141 F. Supp. 3d 1051 (S.D.\nCal. 2015) (citing Restatement of the Law Second, Contracts § 205, Comments a and d (AM. L.\nINST. 1981), and holding that a contract-modification clause was not illusory because the business\nwas bound by the duty of good faith, and that, consequently, the seller \"must exercise this\ndiscretion in a manner consistent with 'the spirit of the bargain' and [consumers'] justified\nexpectations under the contract\") (quoting Scribner v. Worldcom, Inc., 249 F.3d 902, 910 (9th Cir.\n2001)); McKee v. Audible, Inc., 2017 WL 4685039, at *13 (C.D. Cal. July 17, 2017) (holding that\nthe existence of a unilateral modification clause in the business's terms did not render the terms\nsubstantively unconscionable because, inter alia, \"the implied duty of good faith and fair dealing\nrequires [the business] to exercise its discretion in a manner consistent with the justified\nexpectations of the parties\"); Sevier Cnty. Schs. Fed. Credit Union v. Branch Banking & Tr. Co.,\n990 F.3d 470, 478 (6th Cir. 2021) (stating that in Tennessee, when a party makes a unilateral\ncontract change of great magnitude, and especially when that party has \"vastly greater bargaining\npower,\" a court must consider whether the proposed change is, inter alia, \"not in violation of the\nimplied covenant of good faith and fair dealing\"). The duty of good faith also excludes conduct\nthat violates community standards of decency, fairness, or reasonableness.\nc. Interpretation against the drafter. As drafters of consumer contracts, businesses are\nresponsible for any uncertainty, ambiguity, or lack of clear meaning in the contract. Courts have\ntherefore relied on the long-standing principle, restated in Restatement of the Law Second,\nContracts § 206 (AM. L. INST. 1981), that constructs ambiguous contract language against the\ndrafter. See, e.g., Hungate v. Law Off. of David B. Rosen, 391 P.3d 1, 11 (Haw. 2017) (concluding\nthat any ambiguous term should be construed against the drafter, \"or in other words, 'against the\nparty who supplie[s] the words\"') (quoting Restatement of the Law Second, Contracts § 206 (AM.\nL. INST. 1981)); State Sav. & Loan Ass'n v. Kauaian Dev. Co., 613 P.2d 1315, 1322 (Haw. 1980)\n(stating that \"any ambiguity in a mortgage instrument should be construed against the party\ndrawing the documents\"); Corso v. Creighton Univ., 731 F.2d 529, 533 (8th Cir. 1984) (explaining\nthat when \"the contract is on a printed form prepared by one party, and adhered to by another who\nhas little or no bargaining power, ambiguities must be construed against the drafting party\");\nMastrobuono v. Shearson Lehman Hutton, Inc., 514 U.S. 52, 62 (1995) (citing Restatement of the\nLaw Second, Contracts § 206 (AM. L. INST. 1981), in support of the proposition that \"the common-\nlaw rule of contract interpretation [is] that a court should construe ambiguous language against the\n92\n@§4\n"